; DOMAIN: computation/finite-precision-approximation [CLOSED, derived 2026-07-26]
; Claim: Numerical computation studies how continuous mathematics is realized in finite precision, where the achievable accuracy is set by conditioning (problem sensitivity) and stability (algorithm error growth). (1) Every representable number carries relative error ~unit roundoff; error propagates through operations. (2) Conditioning kappa measures how output perturbation amplifies input perturbation, independent of algorithm; a backward-stable algorithm returns the exact answer to a nearby problem, so forward error <= kappa * backward error. (3) Discretization replaces continuous operators (derivatives, integrals) with finite ones, trading truncation error against roundoff; the total error is minimized at an intermediate step size. (4) Iterative methods converge at rates (linear/superlinear/quadratic) governed by spectral properties (contraction factor, condition number); convergence is guaranteed only under stability conditions.
; Structure: (finite precision => bounded relative roundoff per operation) (conditioning kappa = intrinsic sensitivity; stability = algorithm-induced error growth; forward error <= kappa * backward error) (discretization trades truncation vs roundoff, optimal intermediate step) (iterative convergence rate set by spectrum/contraction under stability).
; Result: The reliability of a numerical result is decomposable into a problem-intrinsic factor (conditioning) and an algorithm-intrinsic factor (stability); good numerics means choosing backward-stable algorithms and well-conditioned formulations so finite-precision error stays near the unavoidable roundoff floor.
; Bridge: genesis_computability (numerical methods are the effective procedures that make real-valued computation tractable; finite precision and step counts are the resource bounds on approximating uncomputable/continuous quantities) + genesis_information_geometry (conditioning and error propagation are naturally measured as distances/curvature on the solution manifold; ill-conditioning corresponds to near-degenerate Fisher/metric structure where nearby problems are hard to distinguish).